operator() allows an object to behave like a function

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Square
{
public:
    int operator()(int x)
    {
        return x * x;
    }
};

int main()
{
    Square ob;

    cout << ob(4);

    return 0;
}

ob(4);

Compiler converts it to:

ob.operator()(4);

operator() overloads the function call operator.

It allows an object to behave like a function.

Objects overloading operator() are called Function Objects (Functors). **********************
Suppose you want a custom comparison for sorting.
class Compare
{
public:
    bool operator()(int a, int b)
    {
        return a > b;
    }
};

Compare cmp;

cout << cmp(10, 20);    // Calls cmp.operator()(10,20)

